  <text>
    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="19.2">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:ICn">I Cn</ref> 19.2</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="19.2" corresp="#wit-g-a">⁊ æghwylc unriht aweorpe man georne of þysu<hi rend="italic">m</hi> earde, þæs þe man don mæge,</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="19.2" corresp="#wit-a">And æghwylc unriht aweorpe mann georne of þissum earde, þæs þe man don mage.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="19.2" corresp="#wit-b">⁊ æghwylc unriht aworpe man georne of ðisson earde, ðæs ðe man don mæge.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="19.2" corresp="#wit-q">Omnis iniusticia de finibus nostris, quantum possumus, expellatur.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#robertson" n="19.2" corresp="#wit-b">⁊ æghwylc unriht aworpe man georne of ðisson earde, ðæs ðe man don mæge.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-b">And every injustice shall be zealously cast out from this land, as far as it is possible to do so.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann">Und jedes Unrecht entferne man eifrig aus diesem Lande, so weit als man es zu thun vermag;</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">And let every injustice be eagerly cast out of this country, as far as it can be done;</ab>
        <note type="editorial">Liebermann compares VI Atr 28.1.; Robertson (p. 139) takes I Cn from B, which begins at ‘mæssedæg’ in 14.2, the earlier clauses being supplied from G</note>
      </div>
    </body>
